WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.170
Hey, I'm James Hahne, and this is
PitchGuard XR.

2
00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:08.300
I built it to help coaches spot risky
pitching mechanics using just a phone video.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.550
And these pitching injuries aren't just
one in a thousand.

4
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:16.940
There was a study that followed 261 young
pitchers with an average age of fourteen.

5
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.090
Over the span of ten years, one in four
got a shoulder or elbow injury,

6
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.160
and about one in twenty required surgery
from it.

7
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.670
Pro teams are able to catch these problems
early on with motion capture labs.

8
00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.039
Most youth pitchers don't have access to
that.

9
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.100
What they do have is a phone video, but it's
flat, and a fast arm turns into blur.

10
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.340
Let's look at an example.

11
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.420
If you pause right here, the elbow is not
visible from this angle.

12
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.420
A coach can't tell much from it.

13
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.980
That's why I built PitchGuard XR, to get a
lot more out of that phone video.

14
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:59.130
You film a pitch, and it turns it into a 3D
model you can spin around and slow down.

15
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.150
Here's how it works.

16
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.560
First, the video goes through a program
called Human3R,

17
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.630
which figures out the pitcher's 3D body
and saves it to a data file.

18
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.340
Then, a Python script I wrote reads that
file,

19
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.680
breaks the pitch into six phases, and
scores its joint.

20
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.820
Unity takes all of that and draws it,
which is what you're seeing.

21
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.630
Green means a joint looks fine, and red
means a coach should take a look.

22
00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.360
Watch the elbow turn red right there.

23
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.740
Let's get into the panels.

24
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.210
This is a data panel.

25
00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.730
It shows how the arm is positioned and how much
stress the shoulder and elbow are under

26
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.200
at each moment of the pitch.

27
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.740
It then boils that down into a simple risk
level from low to high.

28
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.570
This is the fatigue monitor.

29
00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.120
It tracks how tired the pitcher is getting
as the pitch goes on.

30
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.790
Lastly, this is the control panel.

31
00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.310
It has a timeline of risk across the whole
pitch,

32
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.790
so you can see exactly where the danger
spikes.

33
00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.530
You can pause, step through frame by
frame,

34
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.710
loop it, or slow it down to take a closer
look.

35
00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.840
The hardest part was the phases.

36
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.390
The 3D data is noisy, so in my Python
code,

37
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.390
a pitch kept flipping back and forth
between phases.

38
00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:06.830
I fixed it two ways.

39
00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.400
I capped joint speeds that were
impossible,

40
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.210
and I made each phase hold for a few
frames before it could switch.

41
00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.760
Then I checked it by hand on thirty-five
clips.

42
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.990
But these scores are just estimates, not
medical advice.

43
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.640
Next, I want to compare them to real lab
motion capture data,

44
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.100
test it with more coaches and pitchers,
and follow pitchers across a season.

45
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.990
I learned how to take messy real-world
data and

46
00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.100
turn it into something a coach can
actually use.

47
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.700
Thanks for watching.
